WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.600
One thing I love in movies 
is miniatures.

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.733
Like, did you know the Poltergeist house 
was only 42 inches (107 cm) wide?

00:00:23.666 --> 00:00:25.933
And the DeLorean from “Back to the Future” 
is a miniature.

00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:28.233
So were the kids in “E.T.”

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:31.166
Okay. Okay. I know 
what you're thinking.

00:00:31.166 --> 00:00:32.666
Those are all old movies.

00:00:32.666 --> 00:00:35.200
Of course they use miniatures, 
ya old grandpa.

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:36.900
And you're not wrong.

00:00:36.900 --> 00:00:42.000
All these films were before that period 
where CGI took over.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.966
I changed to prop making because miniatures,
nobody wanted them anymore.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:51.200
But with Wes Anderson and now 
with some other filmmakers

00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.933
it's strange because they want these...

00:00:53.966 --> 00:00:56.733
Yeah, a bit old-fashioned techniques again.

00:00:56.866 --> 00:00:58.600
I’m Simon Weisse.

00:00:58.600 --> 00:01:01.900
I am a model maker and prop maker.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:05.466
Simon has made props for 
The Matrix Resurrections, Bridge of Spies...

00:01:05.633 --> 00:01:08.633
and he's made miniature models 
for Wes Anderson films like

00:01:08.633 --> 00:01:12.000
Asteroid City, The French Dispatch, 
and The Grand Budapest Hotel.

00:01:12.833 --> 00:01:14.966
Most of the time, if miniatures 
are done right

00:01:14.966 --> 00:01:17.666
the audience isn't supposed to know 
that they're tiny.

00:01:17.933 --> 00:01:18.900
But if you keep an eye out

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.400
 you can see it used 
in a few commonplace.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.800
For expansive sets that establish
the world of a film

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.400
like The Grand Budapest Hotel...

00:01:26.766 --> 00:01:28.600
or the castle from Harry Potter...

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.533
or the opening gates 
of Jurassic World...

00:01:31.900 --> 00:01:34.200
and fantasy vehicles that 
would be too expensive

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.900
or too impossible to build 
at human scale.

00:01:37.166 --> 00:01:40.466
Like the spaceships from E.T.
or Asteroid City

00:01:40.466 --> 00:01:42.433
or the Razor Crest from The Mandalorian.

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:45.533
And if you're trying to blow up 
an entire building for real

00:01:45.533 --> 00:01:47.400
a miniature helps make that possible.

00:01:47.733 --> 00:01:49.500
Like this building from Inception

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:51.500
or the White House from 
Independence Day.

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.666
Though, one thing you 
might have noticed

00:01:53.666 --> 00:01:57.666
is that some of these miniatures 
aren't actually that mini.

00:02:00.166 --> 00:02:02.566
Grand Budapest Hotel is a good example.

00:02:02.566 --> 00:02:05.300
In these times, when we 
talk about miniatures

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:07.666
they are not so small.

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:09.700
I mean, in the 50s and the 60s

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:14.300
you could have a very small house 
and the audience was impressed.

00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:20.000
But today we build those miniatures 
as big as possible.

00:02:20.066 --> 00:02:24.000
We ended up making this in 18th scale,
which is quite big.

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:27.766
That hotel was, in the end,
4 meters (13 feet) wide. 

00:02:27.766 --> 00:02:29.700
I don't know how much this in feet.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:34.233
It was a good scale to have a good definition 
of paint and everything.

00:02:34.333 --> 00:02:36.066
Most of the time we wouldn't

00:02:36.066 --> 00:02:40.166
use the same material as you would 
on a real building

00:02:40.166 --> 00:02:43.033
because if you use lots of bricks

00:02:43.033 --> 00:02:46.200
the structure, it will not be
up to scale.

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:49.066
If there’s wood, we will use wood

00:02:49.066 --> 00:02:54.400
but the very special African woods 
with a very fine structure.

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:57.733
In the end, the thing which 
really changes everything

00:02:57.733 --> 00:02:59.233
is the paint and the patina.

00:02:59.333 --> 00:03:03.400
If they take a big brush, you will see 
the brush on the model.

00:03:03.433 --> 00:03:05.866
No, they have to take 
the finest brush

00:03:05.866 --> 00:03:10.533
and find those dirty pieces near 
the windows and everything.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:13.600
Getting that detail and grunge right

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.200
is especially important to making things 
feel lived in.

00:03:16.466 --> 00:03:20.000
It's one of the reasons that Star Wars 
has such a busy, dirty look.

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:22.833
The more details you have, 
the more realistically

00:03:22.833 --> 00:03:26.300
it can pass as a larger object
when filmed with a camera.

00:03:26.500 --> 00:03:28.900
Choosing the appropriate scale 
to work with, however

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.833
is sometimes a matter of efficiency.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.366
We can work at this 12th and 18th scale. 

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:38.000
I like a lot 35th scale.

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:42.666
That's also because in 18th scale 
and in 35th scale...

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.466
you can find a lot of stuff,
ready made cars

00:03:46.466 --> 00:03:48.700
good looking trees 
and other stuff.

00:03:48.833 --> 00:03:53.600
It takes time off our work, 
which is very long

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:54.966
very time taking.

00:03:55.366 --> 00:03:57.800
Moviemakers can play tricks 
with scale, too

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:00.766
mixing big and small miniatures 
within the same shots.

00:04:00.766 --> 00:04:05.233
And The French Dispatch, we had 
landscapes and, you know

00:04:05.233 --> 00:04:07.600
they were not only in one scale.

00:04:07.866 --> 00:04:10.966
We talk about a forced perspective.

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.300
You had one scale in the foreground.

00:04:13.366 --> 00:04:17.900
Then you get a certain deepness 
to go smaller and smaller and smaller.

00:04:18.066 --> 00:04:21.533
So maybe in the foreground 
you are in 24th scale

00:04:21.533 --> 00:04:24.000
then in the middle 
you’re in 15th scale...

00:04:24.300 --> 00:04:27.133
and in the end you are in the 100th scale.

00:04:27.133 --> 00:04:29.200
And if there's a mountain behind

00:04:29.200 --> 00:04:31.033
it’s in the 1000th scale.

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:37.366
Once they get the scale and
all the details right

00:04:37.366 --> 00:04:39.166
filmmakers still have to make 
this thing look like

00:04:39.166 --> 00:04:41.100
it's the correct size in camera.

00:04:41.333 --> 00:04:44.466
The first issue is how much 
of the frame is in focus.

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:46.766
This is determined by three things:

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:48.333
How long your lens is. 

00:04:48.333 --> 00:04:50.033
How close you are to an object.

00:04:50.200 --> 00:04:52.200
And how much light you're letting 
into the camera.

00:04:52.500 --> 00:04:55.566
The longer your lenses, the more 
compressed an image looks

00:04:55.566 --> 00:04:58.033
and the more parts of the image 
are out of focus.

00:04:58.433 --> 00:05:00.500
Wider lenses are better 
for shooting models

00:05:00.500 --> 00:05:03.666
but to make them look big, 
you have to get really close.

00:05:03.666 --> 00:05:05.466
And when you get really close

00:05:05.466 --> 00:05:08.100
the plane of focus becomes 
extremely shallow...

00:05:08.433 --> 00:05:10.800
meaning only a small bit 
of your frame is in focus

00:05:10.800 --> 00:05:12.300
and everything else is blurry.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:14.700
If you were filming the same shot 
of a real car

00:05:14.700 --> 00:05:18.066
you'd have to be much further away 
to capture that same angle.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.266
And that would make much more 
of the frame in focus.

00:05:21.633 --> 00:05:23.633
Films like “Game Night” 
play with that knowledge

00:05:23.633 --> 00:05:25.966
using strategically blurry photography

00:05:25.966 --> 00:05:27.600
to make this real suburb

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.433
look like a tiny little boardgame.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.466
With miniatures, you want 
the opposite effect.

00:05:32.466 --> 00:05:35.400
Every part of the model should be 
in focus so that it looks big.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:38.366
To do this without changing 
your distance to the model...

00:05:38.533 --> 00:05:41.400
you need to decrease how much light 
you're letting into the camera...

00:05:41.866 --> 00:05:44.000
which makes everything darker.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:46.766
But filming in bright sunlight 
can counteract that issue

00:05:46.766 --> 00:05:49.366
and make the lighting 
feel more realistic overall.

00:05:49.366 --> 00:05:52.866
For other stuff, most of the time 
we are shooting this outside.

00:05:52.866 --> 00:05:55.200
The train and the other stuff.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:58.433
Even The Grand Budapest Hotel 
was shot outside.

00:05:58.633 --> 00:06:00.400
The composition of your model shots 

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.866
matter just as much as 
the technical details.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:05.233
The most striking example 
of this for me

00:06:05.233 --> 00:06:08.066
is the opening shot of the 
original Star Wars.

00:06:08.100 --> 00:06:10.266
By having these two planets
in the frame...

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:12.000
we have an immediate reference point

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.466
that tells us we're working 
on a planetary scale.

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:17.033
As this first ship enters the frame

00:06:17.033 --> 00:06:18.733
we get a sense that this could be

00:06:18.733 --> 00:06:21.100
about the size of a ship 
that carries humans.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:23.400
So when this other ship enters...

00:06:23.566 --> 00:06:26.700
it feels gigantic, menacing, 
and powerful.

00:06:27.166 --> 00:06:28.600
Both models are miniatures

00:06:28.600 --> 00:06:30.700
but how they chose to compose the image

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:32.566
made them feel larger than life.

00:06:35.433 --> 00:06:38.200
Star Wars in 1977 had no choice

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:41.366
but to use miniatures to make 
their futuristic world look real.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.633
Star Wars projects today

00:06:43.633 --> 00:06:45.533
rely heavily on CGI

00:06:45.533 --> 00:06:47.133
like so many other movies.

00:06:47.433 --> 00:06:49.700
But both tools have their strengths.

00:06:50.000 --> 00:06:52.900
Talking about explosions 
and water and everything

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:54.433
I try to avoid miniatures...

00:06:54.866 --> 00:06:57.833
because if you have 
a miniature with water...

00:06:57.933 --> 00:07:00.833
you have to build it at such a big scale

00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:02.900
at 3rd scale or 4th scale.

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:05.200
Otherwise, it doesn't look good.

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:09.600
This is because natural elements 
like water or fire don't scale.

00:07:09.833 --> 00:07:13.733
So while water droplets on 
a full size vehicle look tiny

00:07:13.733 --> 00:07:16.433
those same droplets on a miniature 
would be huge...

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:18.866
giving away that scale 
doesn't match up.

00:07:19.100 --> 00:07:21.433
Sometimes I'm asked, “Oh, could you 
do a miniature?”

00:07:21.433 --> 00:07:24.466
And then we will have water running through.

00:07:24.466 --> 00:07:25.366
And I say, “No....”

00:07:26.433 --> 00:07:27.966
maybe we can do a miniature.

00:07:27.966 --> 00:07:30.800
But the water, please add it digitally.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:33.400
It will look much better 
than real water.

00:07:33.466 --> 00:07:36.066
Explosions can be challenging 
for similar reasons.

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:40.800
This model from “Inception” is truly gigantic 
at four stories (12.19 meters) high...

00:07:40.933 --> 00:07:44.600
and that scale is needed  for 
the explosions to feel more realistic.

00:07:45.433 --> 00:07:48.400
Even if we do those miniature sets...

00:07:48.766 --> 00:07:52.700
It's all in combination with with CGI 
and other new techniques.

00:07:52.733 --> 00:07:55.766
Films like Blade Runner 2049 
and Asteroid City

00:07:55.766 --> 00:07:59.000
use the miniature sets in conjunction 
with real actors

00:07:59.000 --> 00:08:00.633
through the use of greenscreen.

00:08:00.633 --> 00:08:04.166
And The Grand Budapest Hotel showed 
that real humans and puppetry

00:08:04.200 --> 00:08:08.100
could be combined to make this 
delightfully stylized skiing sequence.

00:08:08.733 --> 00:08:11.566
The decision to use miniatures 
varies between directors.

00:08:11.566 --> 00:08:15.766
For Wes Anderson, he believes 
that people are going to recognize

00:08:15.766 --> 00:08:17.600
artificiality, whether it's CG 
or otherwise.

00:08:18.066 --> 00:08:20.400
His tastes lean towards miniatures...

00:08:20.566 --> 00:08:22.866
because he likes the old-fashioned techniques.

00:08:23.333 --> 00:08:25.600
Nolan, on the other hand, 
has used miniatures

00:08:25.600 --> 00:08:29.266
because he wants every scene 
to feel like real footage

00:08:29.266 --> 00:08:31.900
with all the organic messiness 
and artifacting

00:08:31.900 --> 00:08:35.566
that comes with filming a real thing
and having it baked into the shot.

00:08:35.766 --> 00:08:37.733
20 years ago I thought

00:08:37.733 --> 00:08:39.633
miniatures are done, 
it's finished.

00:08:39.633 --> 00:08:41.766
It's all going to be done in CGI...

00:08:41.933 --> 00:08:46.266
but now even in commercials 
they are using miniatures.

00:08:46.300 --> 00:08:48.733
But it's just— I think it's...

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:51.933
for me, it’s an artistic choice.

00:08:52.866 --> 00:08:57.700
The spaceship, which looks very different 
from other spaceships.

00:08:57.766 --> 00:08:59.666
We had so much fun doing this.

00:08:59.666 --> 00:09:01.266
It's a big model.

00:09:01.266 --> 00:09:03.433
Nearly 3 meters (9.8 feet) wide.

00:09:03.766 --> 00:09:08.833
And the spaceship is a mix 
of handmade stuff inside

00:09:08.833 --> 00:09:11.633
and all the sides were laser cut.

00:09:11.833 --> 00:09:14.100
In the end, it was about three months...

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:17.066
because we have all these ongoing process.

00:09:17.066 --> 00:09:18.366
We’ll start with something...

00:09:18.633 --> 00:09:21.900
and sometimes we have 
only two people on this...

00:09:22.100 --> 00:09:25.266
and at the end there are six people on this.

00:09:25.500 --> 00:09:27.800
So yeah, more or less three months.

00:09:27.933 --> 00:09:30.866
For this kind of complicated model.

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:32.866
Yeah, it's about that.

